  "lineage": "Slow-tempo Cuban rumba subform 1850s-onward, the oldest of the three rumba subforms and the precursor parent-form to guaguanco and rumba-columbia. Traditionally played on cajones wooden-shipping-crate-percussion (originating in dockworker-and-warehouse-improvised-drumming) rather than congas, alongside palitos stick-rhythm-and-clave 3-2 wood-block. Performed at funerals, wakes, and elder-honoring contexts, the yambú is the only rumba subform that prohibits the vacunao symbolic-pursuit choreographic-element — the dance is restrained, near-stationary, with subtle-hip-rotational gesture and exaggerated-elderly-mock-tremulous movement. The repertoire preserves the oldest pre-conga rumba percussion vocabulary.",
